# Identity and auth

Wyrd owns identity and authorization on the server. Every request carries exactly
one credential: a Wyrd-signed access token in the `X-Wyrd-Access-Token` header.
The server verifies it, resolves the caller's permissions, and decides what runs.
There is no session cookie and no ambient identity. A client can hold a token, but
only the server decides what it means.

This page is the map. The three pages it links to hold the detail:

- [Authentication](/concepts/authentication/): how each caller obtains a token.
- [Authorization](/concepts/authorization/): how permissions and the policy
  extension gate an action.
- [Cloud identity](/concepts/cloud-identity/): trusted issuers and workload
  bindings for machine identity.

## Terms

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Term', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['Tenant', 'Isolation boundary. Every principal, token, role, issuer, and binding belongs to exactly one tenant. Postgres row-level security enforces it.'],
    ['Principal', 'The identity a token acts as. One of three kinds: User, Service, or Agent.'],
    ['User', 'A human, authenticated through OIDC. Identified by email; no card.'],
    ['Service', 'A non-human principal bound to a ServiceCard. Authenticates with an API key or a workload assertion.'],
    ['Agent', 'A non-human principal bound to an AgentCard. Same auth paths as a Service.'],
    ['Access token', 'Short-lived EdDSA JWT sent on every request. Carries the principal, tenant, and role claims.'],
    ['Refresh token', 'Single-use, rotated token that mints a new access token. Issued only for human login and API-key exchange.'],
    ['Delegated token', 'Access token minted on behalf of another Service/Agent, carrying an act chain. No refresh token.'],
    ['act chain', 'RFC 8693 delegation record embedded in a token: the ordered list of principals that delegated down to the callee.'],
    ['Trusted issuer', 'An external OIDC issuer whose JWTs Wyrd will verify, scoped to one tenant.'],
    ['Workload binding', 'Server-owned mapping (issuer, subject, audience) → CardRef that names which Service/Agent a workload assertion represents.'],
    ['RBAC', 'Role-based access control. A principal\'s roles resolve to an effective permission set of { resource, action, scope } entries.'],
    ['PolicyHook', 'Pluggable policy-evaluation seam that gates the /v1/authz/check endpoint before RBAC.']
  ]}
/>

## The authenticated request path

Once a caller has an access token, every request follows the same path. The
server never trusts the token's contents until `TokenVerifier::verify` has
checked them; the tenant is read from the unverified token only to route to the
correct tenant verifier, not to grant anything.

<WyrdSequenceDiagram
  title="Authenticated request verification"
  participants={['Client', 'Wyrd server', 'Postgres']}
  messages={[
    { from: 'Client', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'X-Wyrd-Access-Token: Bearer <jwt>' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'tenant from unverified token → route' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'verify sig · kid · iss=wyrd · exp · tenant · revocation' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Postgres', label: 'resolve roles → effective_permissions' },
    { from: 'Postgres', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'permissions', dashed: true },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Client', label: 'AuthenticatedPrincipal → handler runs', dashed: true }
  ]}
/>

`TokenVerifier::verify` checks five things: the EdDSA signature against the key
named by the token's `kid`, the `iss=wyrd` claim, expiry (with a configured clock
skew), the tenant binding, and the principal's revocation epoch. A token issued
before the principal's `tokens_not_before` is rejected. The verifier then resolves
the token's role claims to an effective permission set through
`SqlPermissionResolver`. The handler receives an `AuthenticatedPrincipal` and never
re-parses the raw token.

## Obtaining a token: `/auth/token` grant dispatch

All machine-facing token issuance goes through one route, `POST /auth/token`,
which matches on the `TokenRequest` grant and dispatches to the right exchange.
Human login is the exception: it starts at `GET /auth/login` and finishes with the
`authorization_code` grant on the same route.

<WyrdFlowDiagram
  variant="fan"
  title="/auth/token grant dispatch"
  steps={[
    { label: 'POST /auth/token', sub: 'match TokenRequest' },
    { label: 'wyrd_api_key', sub: 'API key → access + refresh' },
    { label: 'jwt-bearer', sub: 'workload assertion → access only' },
    { label: 'authorization_code', sub: 'OIDC callback → access + refresh' },
    { label: 'refresh_token', sub: 'rotate → access + refresh' },
    { label: 'token-exchange', sub: 'delegation → access only · preview-gated' }
  ]}
/>

Who uses which grant:

- **Humans** log in through OIDC (`authorization_code`) and receive **User**
  tokens.
- **Services and Agents** authenticate with an API key (`wyrd_api_key`) or a
  workload assertion (`jwt-bearer`).
- **Delegation** (`token-exchange`) lets a caller mint a scoped token for
  another Service/Agent. It is gated behind preview auth and never issues a
  refresh token. See [Authorization](/concepts/authorization/#delegation).

The per-grant flows, with diagrams, are on the
[Authentication](/concepts/authentication/) page.

## Authorization and delegation

Authentication answers *who is calling*. Authorization answers *may they do
this*. After a token verifies, its role claims resolve to an effective
permission set, and RBAC checks the requested permission — operation *and*
object scope — against it.

Agent-to-agent calls add a second layer. A caller with `delegation:issue` can
exchange its token for a **delegated** Service/Agent token that carries an `act`
chain. Only delegated tokens reach `POST /v1/authz/check`, the policy-extension
endpoint backed by `PolicyHook`. Both layers must pass: the `PolicyHook` first,
then RBAC. See [Authorization](/concepts/authorization/).

## Development vs production auth

The deployment profile selects hardened or relaxed defaults. `APP_ENV=development`
selects `DeploymentProfile::Development`; `staging` and `production` both select
the hardened production profile. An unrecognized value fails boot.

The signing key is the difference that matters most.

In **development**, if no signing key is configured, the server mints an ephemeral
Ed25519 key at boot so auth works on a fresh local run. Those tokens do not survive
a restart, and the key must never appear in staging or production.

In **production** (staging or production), boot fails closed without
`WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` or `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_PEM`. It also rejects
`auth.allow_preview=true` and refuses to boot with the default `StubAllowPolicyHook`
or the no-op audit writer still mounted.

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Variable', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Effect on auth' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['APP_ENV', 'development | staging | production. Selects the deployment profile; staging and production both harden.'],
    ['WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE', 'Path to the Ed25519 signing-key PEM (preferred; keeps the key out of the process environment). Required in production.'],
    ['WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_PEM', 'Inline Ed25519 signing-key PEM (fallback). Setting both file and inline is a configuration error.'],
    ['WYRD_AUTH_ALLOW_PREVIEW', 'Enables preview grants (token-exchange delegation). Rejected in a production profile.'],
    ['WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG', 'Implicit tenant slug used to seed trusted issuers/bindings at boot and to resolve the login tenant.'],
    ['WYRD_SEALING_KEY_FILE', 'AES-256-GCM key file for OIDC client-secret encryption at rest. See Cloud identity.'],
    ['WYRD_SEALING_KEY_BASE64', 'Inline base64 sealing key (fallback for the file form).']
  ]}
/>

<Aside title="Server config vs workspace config">

Server auth runtime config lives in the `wyrd-server` crate (`config.rs`): the
signing key, sealing key, deployment profile, tenant slug, trusted issuers, and
workload bindings. The separate `wyrd-config` crate handles workspace `wyrd.toml`
defaults for cards; it is not the server auth runtime config. Do not look for auth
settings there.

</Aside>

